<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pomerelia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gdańsk Pomerania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pomerelia, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, is a historical region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> west of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, centred on Gdańsk. Ruled by native dukes until 1294, it passed to Poland and was seized by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1308. It returned to Poland as part of Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1466, was annexed by Prussia in the partitions and became the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-corridor">Polish Corridor</a> after 1919.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pomerelia.jpg" alt="Pomerelia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kapitel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torun_rynek_staromiejski_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pomerelia lies west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and east of the Łeba, mostly within the Pomeranian Voivodeship. It is traditionally divided into Kashubia, Kociewie, Tuchola Forest, the Vistula Fens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>, inhabited by Kashubians, Kociewians, Borowians and Chełminians. The name means small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and has been used since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> to distinguish it from Hither and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/further-pomerania">Farther Pomerania</a> to the west. Its earliest appearance is in the Pomerellische Handveste of 1441, issued by the Teutonic Grandmaster Konrad von Ehrlichshausen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was settled by West Slavic and Lechitic tribes in the 7th and 8th centuries. In the tenth century it was incorporated into early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> or perhaps by his father, and Mieszko founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> between 970 and 980 to control the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>. Its ties with Poland loosened in the eleventh century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> re-established direct control in 1116. After 1138 local governors of the Samborides dynasty became semi-independent, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliwa-cathedral">Oliwa Abbey</a> as the Christian centre.</p><p>In 1210 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II of Denmark</a> invaded and Mestwin I became his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, but Mestwin's son Swietopelk II gained full independence in 1227. He chartered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law and settled his conflicts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, his eastern neighbour from 1230, in 1253. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kepno">Treaty of Kępno</a> of 15 February 1282 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestwin-ii">Mestwin II</a> transferred suzerainty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and kept control until his death in 1294.</p><p>After a revolt of the nobility in 1300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a> took the region, and Władysław I the Elbow-high recaptured it in 1306. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, called in against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, kept it for themselves, and the dispute led to Polish–Teutonic Wars through the 14th and 15th centuries. In 1454 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> agreed to re-incorporate the region, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteen-years-war-1454-1466">Thirteen Years' War (1454–1466)</a> the Order renounced its claims. It became the Pomeranian Voivodeship within Royal Prussia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> was the largest port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, exporting mostly grain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Historic Pomerelia forms the bulk of the Pomeranian Voivodeship, with its southern part in the Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship and a small western fragment in the West Pomeranian Voivodeship. Its largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. Prussia annexed it in the Partitions of Poland, and most of it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after 1918 and again in 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pomerelia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pomerelia</b>, originally Polish, became a fully independent duchy in 1227 and was seized by the Teutonic Knights in 1309, after Władysław I the Elbow-high had asked them for help against Brandenburg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1308 the knights seized Danzig and <b>Pomerelia</b>, and in 1309 the Grand Master moved his seat from Venice to Marienburg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Przemysl II gained <b>Pomerelia</b> in 1294 and was crowned King of Poland in Gniezno in 1295.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>He relied on support from the Teutonic Order and the dukes of <b>Pomerelia</b>, which caused revolts among his own nobles.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-inowroclaw">Duchy of Inowroclaw</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pomerelia</b> controlled the mouth of the Vistula, and Gdansk grew into a busy port with German, Slavic and Danish merchants.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerelia">Duchy of Pomerelia</a></li></ul></section>
